1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,500
They say a Chinese ship
needs no nails at all.

2
00:00:05,500 --> 00:00:14,000
A romantic line — and only half true.

3
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:22,000
One timber keeps a tongue;
the other cuts the socket.

4
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:30,000
No glue, no nails.
The shape itself holds.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:38,000
But a ship goes into the water
and stays wet for decades.

6
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:46,000
So: tenons locate, nails lock,
tung-oil putty and hemp seal the seam.

7
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:56,000
Twelve bulkheads split the hull
into thirteen sealed holds.

8
00:00:56,000 --> 00:01:05,000
One hold floods.
The ship lists, then settles.

9
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:14,000
Take the bulkheads out and the water
runs the length of the hull.

10
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:24,000
English has no word for 榫卯.
The closest is mortise-and-tenon joint.

11
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:30,000
Wood holds wood. Nails hold wood.
Words hold words.
